// The debug world's time of day: the standard's day cycle (skies.puck) on the hall's `key` sun, a 24-minute day that
// starts at ten in the morning, with the noon sun 46 degrees up toward +x and -z. A 24-minute day turns the sun a
// quarter of a degree a second, slow enough to watch the shadows creep and fast enough that a session sees a sunset.
// At night a deep-blue moon of 6 lux, brighter than a real full moon so the valley reads, lights it opposite the sun,
// under a night sky of 1 nit and its stars; the hall's exposure floor drops with the day to meet it (hall.puck).
// debug.md's "Time of day" gives the levers: write `day` to move the time, and its advance to hold the sun.

import "../standard/skies"
// The hall keys its exposure floor on the day.
exports {
  reads [day]
}

dayCycle(daySeconds: 1440, start: 10, noonElevation: 46, heading: 37, cloudiness: 0.3, moonPeak: 6, moonColor: "#7088E8", nightLuminance: 1, starLuminance: 3)
